se points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/ef/ef5f33b74b59b5a62fd8670a8feb6487b01a97aed4a44a098a576d10d891bd68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f928683-2206-515e-9154-238c2b97ff67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257621} {:answer/id #uuid "771aae05-3e43-53e5-a66d-413cfd014971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257834} {:answer/id #uuid "8401c7a8-8de7-538a-b715-56aeb8b85245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257952} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f47d0b-3af1-5625-9eef-20a43b3c90a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258194} {:answer/id #uuid "baf66338-ec48-5785-9b89-dee745803bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot k!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "771aae05-3e43-53e5-a66d-413cfd014971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257834} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b168bd-c6b6-557b-94bf-969806cf924b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06937889-7753-54db-a2ad-60dda70867ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-124142" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k+2)!$ for any $k\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+2)! & =(k+2)\\cdot \\overset{(k+1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d084334-b89a-5a33-9373-086018cdfbc3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Factorial of Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639bcea5-e2bf-5810-bd1d-be75cc7c83f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257658} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e498dc-6116-583e-9426-bf9c965b7848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258344} {:answer/id #uuid "f2acdf55-5a3c-5fa6-a227-9905caee424d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258896} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cce177-3888-5e25-b5bb-13548db279fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "119" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258911} {:answer/id #uuid "feaa5d9d-3191-5d57-9370-7daa98ad50d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3cce177-3888-5e25-b5bb-13548db279fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "119" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258911} :answer-field/id #uuid "380c5250-bbac-5f1f-9d84-134985995235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d53009d8-333f-5b2e-8589-e17e64e488ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-124405" :question/problem "$5!-0!=$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a subtraction of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5!=5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=120.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5!-0! & =120-1=119\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083335 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d084334-b89a-5a33-9373-086018cdfbc3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Factorial of Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31276a86-fa0a-5124-9a34-35deb513b5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257216} {:answer/id #uuid "7286a7ec-06d1-5505-a378-32bc43e56f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257790} {:answer/id #uuid "ace16a38-1aab-5b1c-be7b-7b5d2490143e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258290} {:answer/id #uuid "b411a73c-fb57-5b2e-8277-a2e682ee5da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258346} {:answer/id #uuid "cd77c09b-0289-579c-80e8-ca32a260ca31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b411a73c-fb57-5b2e-8277-a2e682ee5da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258346} :answer-field/id #uuid "c80ea1ab-d67b-53ca-86c4-fea394be3dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf7bd909-cf27-508b-8907-199d014908fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124410" :question/problem "$3!-0!=$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a subtraction of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3!=3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3!-0! & =6-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27147d26-f01c-583d-935d-f91c78e5041f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257121} {:answer/id #uuid "286fec25-4908-5694-a681-f2ea16ea8ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257142} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce385bc-2516-5301-a4cb-0ce65a749f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258162} {:answer/id #uuid "b22bab3f-9316-5310-8fb4-b670b1d10da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258333} {:answer/id #uuid "f1239ea4-4271-56cc-bc8e-fe1a2fd69654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22bab3f-9316-5310-8fb4-b670b1d10da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258333} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b4e062-0d53-5765-889c-2a0da239ec0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cbd5c1c-b243-5354-99fa-1a41e7c9ebe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124475" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0ad5279a7ec9ac20a2c4e1449f93ae67854caf021aba8435165b80069f23afbd.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠B={60}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠B,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{AC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+2) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+2\\sqrt{3} & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2=4\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+2=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fae7a58-5f8a-59f7-a26b-e37ea84449ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258044} {:answer/id #uuid "ac295ae4-0956-533a-95bb-43fe80dfb821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258282} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c69797-b7c1-5c9b-8c12-6254b4fc206c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258358} {:answer/id #uuid "c63b6375-a6af-5c2b-bb3b-f9ac6aca359c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+1)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258515} {:answer/id #uuid "efaa4806-083a-546d-8433-3487b50984f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac295ae4-0956-533a-95bb-43fe80dfb821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258282} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03fcfaa-96c1-5bd9-a600-2d70d1ca8a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12e390ab-b9d1-50cd-bae8-a1a0dd93fc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-124498" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+3)!$ for any $n\\ge -1?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+3)! & =(n+3)\\cdot \\overset{(n+2)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037a7c54-c302-5789-b25e-bd233adb82e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256811} {:answer/id #uuid "0bf43ace-4823-5eaa-9ad1-0be3fc1525b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898} {:answer/id #uuid "45b120b9-4ece-5a16-a391-5bfbb0ea9f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257392} {:answer/id #uuid "ad55cbe1-d31f-5103-bded-f0845d5ae71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258296} {:answer/id #uuid "c5881a22-3400-5139-bc65-47b813f402ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf43ace-4823-5eaa-9ad1-0be3fc1525b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898} :answer-field/id #uuid "9474a253-0604-56e8-84bf-d3c9bd812162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7d8485d-2f78-5d59-9916-1d1cd2c548de" :question/math-academy-id "q-124608" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11b11b-3274-5c34-b09c-f5ec78de4e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257177} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcebdf8-f732-50f7-b15a-29792fc1f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257450} {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} {:answer/id #uuid "5d59303b-e2ee-5904-a3d2-9f17c7fcfaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257597} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb144b3-687e-5d43-94a0-00b76d2854a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd602cdd-cbaf-5de8-8895-9b474285c26d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66205715-ba82-541e-8ac0-31ae0009bc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-124611" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ae39b9-8d77-5506-95cb-36cfda6b93ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258226} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ca8282-18dd-559f-b3d0-161a4153c226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258329} {:answer/id #uuid "f50445f6-c5b6-5037-8d82-ae09d76d2629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258921} {:answer/id #uuid "f57c9ed2-c68c-53ab-85fa-4cb0d40fff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} :answer-field/id #uuid "62707eaf-bd66-53e1-b24c-8b731568b056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "186cddc1-d1ed-556b-9213-9d3efc27c116" :question/math-academy-id "q-124743" :question/problem "![](images/02/02cb693b74f0f315389f87ceed1674da11f661b3363e4df0890bdf82d61cc60c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a4/a479e8e7906a3a0dc43e925c5bccd6534b4ab2e02a289403b2d0c92d0b0eb1d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148d9fa6-e709-5ed3-8dee-a9794d49d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256967} {:answer/id #uuid "57004998-41ca-5e4e-90f5-2c24561a08c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee702e6-7a9d-5c39-9f0f-93b8b0fe4698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} {:answer/id #uuid "d244da59-6698-5edd-a3a0-8e822fc76895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615} {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654efa3-cd29-5e20-bbaf-04bd03e93e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba2d6f5-ab4c-55d2-aafd-8aaa2c0dbeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "374059a8-6fd5-53d5-82e6-c0ca083f7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257267} {:answer/id #uuid "605bf4ad-ad76-590d-bd34-06fd61323e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257629} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca6d0e9-6ce5-54df-867d-65c15a711c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258017} {:answer/id #uuid "9e56d472-5870-5827-b0a4-e0552ef27936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258174} {:answer/id #uuid "a6765343-206b-5392-8a7c-8689b6c899f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6765343-206b-5392-8a7c-8689b6c899f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258234} :answer-field/id #uuid "affc45c9-4666-5a81-994f-df2750e5a840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0991dc0-bba8-5452-beb4-9ef876fd9e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-124816" :question/problem "![](images/36/3687e362fea73defca43332ee70f232282ad1eded9dc14cbbc045de4324067f0.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{6}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3064ab7-54be-5462-ae53-ab47118bf7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387611} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7e2f0c-1e2b-5ea8-be50-daf51e2e9626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387612} {:answer/id #uuid "d7168e0d-4c71-55fb-b5dc-472312e878ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387613} {:answer/id #uuid "fa744589-a280-5c0b-adc9-a65dd97443bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+120{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387614} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bf4816-a669-55ac-a5bd-36b3025ebd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3064ab7-54be-5462-ae53-ab47118bf7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387611} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b383894-df41-50ee-a309-d940d6accc1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e01d5c53-05d5-5886-b4aa-a254711880e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-12490" :question/problem "${(x+7y)}^{10}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ $n=10.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x+7y)}^{10}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10} & =(\\frac{10}{0}){(x)}^{10}{(7y)}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){(x)}^{10-1}{(7y)}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){(x)}^{10-2}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{10}+10{(x)}^{9}{(7y)}^{1}+45{(x)}^{8}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1({x}^{10})+10({x}^{9})(7y)+45({x}^{8})(49{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14557b18-0563-5b53-9f86-fa3ca7f47384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256963} {:answer/id #uuid "655311f9-e5d1-5870-9895-37bfc0e5bf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257671} {:answer/id #uuid "764ac68c-5a27-54cd-b524-a31f92868b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257820} {:answer/id #uuid "82af07e0-822b-5ca8-9bee-31fb9e99ddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257940} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb1be87-9bec-5261-a137-45f33fdea218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "764ac68c-5a27-54cd-b524-a31f92868b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257820} :answer-field/id #uuid "6318d8bf-6cd1-5740-986d-80321788e4a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5732cef-fd51-5b54-b80c-b4a2a52357b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124958" :question/problem "If $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sin⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e887638bb8b3b135c6a8e6eecf564fe8310a43f2ce26f8b565ec6966bafb89c.png)\n\nWe're given that $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283723ea-a88e-58ae-b5e7-50b3adc30b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257138} {:answer/id #uuid "554dab01-2781-5b3b-904c-6f7365f441e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257512} {:answer/id #uuid "a9807580-b8ed-5e0b-9685-81cacc71955c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258262} {:answer/id #uuid "cba11a29-a963-50dc-95d2-18ae2dc7ff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258547} {:answer/id #uuid "f47fdff0-7ad2-5bd4-8cd6-52ab13350e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba11a29-a963-50dc-95d2-18ae2dc7ff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258547} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e570dc4-28db-5cee-8f25-148cbb584b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f344f8f2-1c82-536a-b6c4-df0e2bd726d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124959" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f6/f6c2b0f2e6b8c9b10de0341a73d8c7d1d88ba64bd046e82fee8db37828ca2555.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439a2aa-fc95-5a6f-8ba6-a08e185beaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256815} {:answer/id #uuid "76cdbd0e-31fa-5d43-9da9-087458569c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257830} {:answer/id #uuid "7a976b23-fe40-5cb5-af5e-04ee0fd15dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257866} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4cbcec-1f82-59d7-95a8-2396d64d3e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258449} {:answer/id #uuid "cb446671-4285-5ffd-806b-31e615004133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76cdbd0e-31fa-5d43-9da9-087458569c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257830} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ede77b-ed26-5e38-bf4c-454ee0d7c02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "395d59bc-74f3-5209-b445-2d063af44516" :question/math-academy-id "q-12496" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{3}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the ${x}^{3}$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{4}+11).$ But the derivative is $4{x}^{3},$ so we're only out by a constant factor. \n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{4}+11.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{4}+11)^{9}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{9}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{9}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{u}^{10}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({x}^{4}+11)^{10}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1e516a-fd02-539c-a55b-1d53b977ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400158} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd19dff-a905-5735-9496-24d7b4eee41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400159} {:answer/id #uuid "7175eeec-eda1-5a92-99be-f76806feffc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400160} {:answer/id #uuid "14302d62-f56c-5807-8418-d9e29a49a8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400161} {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec6cfae-f16c-5f01-8f87-2f00e0b258ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb1887b-828c-58c3-9b10-3c0e7dc6d40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124965" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd41c969aee2e7c04c9034106978d8522d6a715733098bfde87abbdb861da7a8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00a40ba-01e3-5ee1-8e78-5ca100de2c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395730} {:answer/id #uuid "b95804dd-01ed-5465-b1ec-454869fcea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395731} {:answer/id #uuid "957bc594-a52d-54c5-8fe2-348b94cdf190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395732} {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} {:answer/id #uuid "2458c64b-d709-5fd5-a6bf-7d12e7bb36e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf9bf65-124a-5233-aedc-81c57617266f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "457b9ae0-36a8-577c-85ad-8057dc66fc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12498" :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x+1)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbeb0313-5651-561b-a4b7-42ac4f88f1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400165} {:answer/id #uuid "1a165698-e8f8-506c-9756-3955aee8c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400166} {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3e631f-f822-5565-8077-5a496dd0604b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400168} {:answer/id #uuid "0944c75f-3871-5029-b2ac-06fe51638caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} :answer-field/id #uuid "77272688-3fb6-5eaf-8b2d-59ba189820e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c006e542-29a2-5102-8308-98f4920ebd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-125004" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/5c/5c98414bbe0e1e373864e9ec1d6b8667bb0032c8824f20bed1e6f9b9079c4d02.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8})}=-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453def53-174d-5896-8e4f-7faa7466d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{({x}^{2}+5)}^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257385} {:answer/id #uuid "98eb7e12-8b7e-5878-badc-a9f99309abb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258129} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b2fef7-0445-5ee0-9c9b-2663d3fb7feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258199} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcefc88-d3ad-5c21-9ac6-75e0800e4041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258438} {:answer/id #uuid "dfeda5a9-a962-51e6-b8b1-1e93608cc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfeda5a9-a962-51e6-b8b1-1e93608cc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7f8515-44cd-5765-b9d4-3675220879f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d839b9a-ccae-57dc-9109-2e848ce0d27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12502" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+5.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{10}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9{u}^{9}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18{u}^{9}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18({x}^{2}+5)^{9}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceb3c00-a293-5837-a92d-52eeb50f0ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400187} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca2de-1a70-5b67-a36e-991a07dbc939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400188} {:answer/id #uuid "9f38d46b-1ede-55d8-a14d-a43663a75ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400189} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab42314-64ed-5e2f-b8e6-3a69d859b6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} :answer-field/id #uuid "441fdc82-6754-52e3-ae25-4987eecf3c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b30b956-8f97-5e5a-bc88-28b6d6148226" :question/math-academy-id "q-125549" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d783281-8364-5597-a804-a4ea0d76bec8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7d1ec58-4625-5961-a2e3-b5cd7345701a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398265} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bfa313-dae4-53f3-a57b-64e8da4543c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398266} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0fb717-3703-5718-a040-1535e3177723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398267} {:answer/id #uuid "b38fc1a7-079a-5f48-a57c-e34f53ec5e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398268} {:answer/id #uuid "6542246e-6fb1-5a0a-8c66-d92b07a647e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7bfa313-dae4-53f3-a57b-64e8da4543c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398266} :answer-field/id #uuid "21415ffd-ab5b-5844-9b2a-182f8ede2fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8904abf3-dbf2-549a-8f8b-9fbdb7308ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125561" :question/problem "Find the entry ${e}_{31}$ of the matrix $E$, where \n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry ${e}_{31}$ lies at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $1$st column. Therefore, ${e}_{31}=-3.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
